<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lichfield Gospels</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as St Chad Gospels, Book of Saint Chad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> are an 8th-century illuminated gospel book in the Insular style, kept at Lichfield Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In the 9th century it was at Llandeilo Fawr in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, and notes written in its margins there include some of the earliest surviving written Welsh. It reached Lichfield by the late 10th century and is also known as the St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> Gospels.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lichfield-gospels.jpg" alt="Lichfield Gospels" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_St_Luke,_St_Chad_Gospels.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Scholars name four places where the book may have been made: Ireland, Northumbria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a> itself. Its script and painting techniques link it to Northumbria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> and resemble those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne-gospels">Lindisfarne Gospels</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-kells">Book of Kells</a>; the art historian Peter Lord dates it to 730, after the first and before the second. On style alone it is placed between 698 and 800.</p><p>Some scholars read its Welsh marginalia as a sign that it was written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, perhaps at Llandeilo Fawr, but Wendy Stein argued for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a> in 1980, Pamela James reached the same conclusion in 1996 from its materials and script, and the discovery of the Lichfield Angel in 2003 added support. Without definitive evidence the question remains open.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The 236 surviving pages measure 30.8 cm by 23.5 cm; eight are illuminated and four more contain framed text. They hold the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> of Matthew and Mark and the early part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in a single column, based on the Vulgate but with almost 2000 variances from it, nearly a third of them shared with the Hereford Gospels. The script is predominantly Insular majuscule with some uncial features and is therefore called semi-uncial. Its regularity suggests a single scribe, though some evidence points to as many as four.</p><p>The decoration includes portraits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-the-evangelist">St Mark</a> and St Luke, a carpet page, incipit pages for Matthew, Mark and Luke, a Chi Rho monogram page and a page with the four symbols of the evangelists. Matthew's incipit page is badly worn, as if it had served for years as the front cover.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The first of eight marginal inscriptions records in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> the gift of the book to God on the altar of St Teilo by a man named Gelhi, who had bought it from Cingal for the price of his best horse. The second preserves a unique example of early Welsh prose, recording the settlement of a land dispute; both are dated to the mid-9th century. A faded signature on the opening folio, Wynsige presul, probably refers to the Wynsige who was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a> from about 963 to 972–5.</p><p>In 1646, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>, Lichfield Cathedral was sacked and its library looted. The books went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">Frances</a>, Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, who returned them in 1672 or 1673, and the second volume of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> was probably lost then. The book was put on public display in 1982, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Lichfield still swear allegiance to the Crown on it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lichfield Gospels” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Earlier members include the Cathach of St. Columba and the Book of Durrow; the early 8th century brought the Lindisfarne Gospels, the Echternach Gospels and the <b>Lichfield Gospels</b>, and the Book of Armagh is dated to 807–809.</blockquote><a href="/work/book-of-kells">Book of Kells · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tywi valley was among the best farmland in south-west Wales, with major church sites such as Llandeilo Fawr, home of the gospel book now known as the <b>Lichfield Gospels</b> before it left Wales.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ystrad-tywi">Kingdom of Ystrad Tywi</a></li></ul></section>
